    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Aftershocks, both at Landers and elsewhere, have been correlated spatially with changes to the maximum Coulomb stress due to fault slip. The orientations of individual aftershocks have been mechanically related to this perturbed stress field, with distinct fault geometries best reproducing the local stress tensor at the hypocenters of large aftershocks. We present analysis of aftershocks from the M7.3 Landers earthquake that occurred in southern California in 1992 and demonstrate changes to the local stress field for two different model geometries of the Landers faults, determined with mechanical forward models.\r\n\r\nKnowing the remote stress state is critical for driving these forward models, in addition to being critical to understanding regional tectonics. Yet the far field stress remains poorly constrained in many locations, including at Landers. In addition to borehole breakouts and hydraulic fracturing, the inversion of aftershock focal mechanisms is a means for determining a homogeneous remote field. However, inversion methods generally overlook the relationships between aftershocks, stress perturbations, and fault geometry. New software from IGEOSS (now a Schlumberger company), iBem3D, improves upon previous inversion algorithms by solving the complete mechanical problem, attributing aftershocks to the remote stress field through slip on model faults.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
